\f1\fs22 Gerald Hardman\'97\'93Gerry\'94 at home, \'93Very\'94 among agency colleagues\'97hurried from the hover-copt, across the pad, tugging his mask tighter and bracing his mackintosh. The morning was squally, whipping peaks of the Layer into little whitecaps that wafted over the low hover-pad sprouts. Gerry held his breath until he was safely inside.\
As usual, the main foyer at TRAMPS Tower was as reverently quiet as a cathedral, if not half as sincere. Busy dark suits, imposing uniforms, and sensibly cut skirts glided through, shoes scuffing and clicking across the ever-shifting CEO hologram projected on the floor. It was impossible to cross the foyer without at least one eye on you, especially Piotr Dunhilton\'92s, larger than life.\
Gerry kept up his veneer of hushed awe until past the security turnstiles, the detect-everything gates, into the sullen, faintly chemical elevator lobby. Down he went. For all his brisk progress as a middle manager (under the careful engineering of his PAPER masters), his offices never made it above the Layer. This latest posting came with a corner office whose panoramic view consisted of alternating swirls of septic yellow-white and hospital-green corrosion\'97glass so tarnished Gerry might as well be looking in a necrotic mirror.\
He almost pitied the Cloudborne cleaners who\'92d have to suit up and replace the glazing, but reminded himself, they were Cloudborne. If they cared to improve their station in life, they should have\'97so he owed them nothing.\
Gerry dumped his tablet on his desk, flicked off the lights, and squashed his nose against the window. The view never changed.\
His tablet binged.\
\'93Excuse me, sir,\'94 the floor secretary announced.\
\'93What?\'94 Gerry answered, still nose to glass, nose sounding as blocked as his prospects.\
\'93You have a meeting in the Commissioner\'92s office.\'94\
\'93When?\'94\
\'93Immediately.\'94\
\
\'93You can go right in,\'94 the Commissioner\'92s assistant constable said, her tone so unexpectedly warm that Gerry immediately distrusted it. Maybe his double-life was done for. He pushed at the Commissioner\'92s great, theatrical door, too gently; it didn\'92t budge. He tried again, with more shoulder, and entered blinking.\
Brilliance. The Commissioner\'92s multi-storey windows flooded the office with sunlight so blinding Gerry had to squint so hard he could not determine whether anyone was sitting in the great officious chair behind the great officious desk, or the identity of the square-headed silhouette standing alongside it in a coronet of blaring rainbow light.\
\'93Come in,\'94 a voice called\'97not the Commissioner\'92s, but familiar.\
Gerry crept the long expanse to the desk, realising the standing shadow radiated PAPER from every pore. Saviour, or cosigner on his execution?\
A figure emerged from the shadows. \'93Have a seat,\'94 he said, gesturing at the Commissioner\'92s chair.\
